@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+# Cooperative launch activation controls
+
+`make activation-relay` builds small native applications and exercises the actual
+activation relay with only the two Steam frontend identities replaced by private
+test identities. It never loads code into Steam or launches a game. The activation
+source uses production memory-management flags; the test app uses ARC. The
+parent invokes the shipped `activation-request` entry point through a shell,
+keeping its main queue available as Steam does after spawning the tool.
+
+Run on an unlocked macOS desktop without another Wine session. The controls change
+focus for about ninety seconds; do not interact with their windows. Each application exits
+on its own. Cases wait for their exact private executable paths to disappear;
+missing asynchronous launch completion fails cleanup. Failure cleanup signals
+only those paths, never an application name or shared bundle identifier. Each
+suite uses private frontend identifiers, and workspace launches request fresh
+instances. Receipts, source/binary hashes and native callback logs are retained in
+`out/activation-*`. Missing callbacks, unexpected application switches and incomplete
+results fail rather than being counted as success.
+
+The detached no-handoff baseline must remain inactive. A ready helper must grant
+activation for immediate and delayed windows. Blocking the helper's main queue
+must demonstrate acknowledgement before spawning, not success by a repeated lucky
+timing. An absent receiver must time out within the bounded interval and continue
+without activation. A deliberate switch during delayed creation must keep the
+other app active. Requests, acknowledgements, application activation and key-window
+callbacks are recorded separately. A foreground-parent control also checks the
+request while the client's own main queue receives it. Controlled helper startup
+and termination delays exercise the exit wait before another case begins.
+
+`make activation-processes` exercises the bounded exit wait, timeout and failure
+cleanup without changing desktop focus. A same-named executable outside the case
+must survive, and an unaccounted asynchronous launch must fail cleanup.
+Hadron's `tests/mac/test-steam-activation.py` exercises the real spawn hook and
+`steam-run` routing with inert backends: direct and shell-wrapped games/launchers,
+quoted arguments, app-ID precedence/fallback, path queries and outside-install
+helpers. No game, Wine, or activation API is invoked by those routing tests.
+
+These controls reproduce the native handoff mechanism. They do not establish game
+rendering, input, fullscreen presentation, Wine minimization behavior, or acceptance
+on a different macOS version. Validate those through ordinary owner-led Steam
+launches with the installed candidate and again without temporary diagnostics.
